WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.275
When a workflow must accept a URL,

2
00:00:02.355 --> 00:00:05.842
parse it once with a standards-aware API
such as Node's URL class.

3
00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:10.170
A URL is structured data: protocol,
username, password, hostname, port, path,

4
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.659
query, and fragment have distinct
meanings.

5
00:00:12.739 --> 00:00:16.924
Security rules should inspect those parsed
components rather than search the

6
00:00:17.004 --> 00:00:17.955
original string.

7
00:00:18.035 --> 00:00:22.536
Raw prefix tests confuse textual
resemblance with origin identity and can approve a

8
00:00:22.616 --> 00:00:25.025
hostname that merely contains trusted
text.

9
00:00:25.105 --> 00:00:27.451
Begin with an explicit protocol allowlist.

10
00:00:27.531 --> 00:00:30.067
A web retrieval feature generally needs
HTTPS,

11
00:00:30.147 --> 00:00:33.571
or HTTPS and HTTP when the business
requirement justifies both.

12
00:00:33.651 --> 00:00:35.680
Reject every other scheme by default.

13
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.451
Also reject embedded usernames and
passwords unless the protocol contract explicitly

14
00:00:40.531 --> 00:00:41.355
requires them.

15
00:00:41.435 --> 00:00:44.542
Credentials inside a URL complicate
logging, redaction,

16
00:00:44.622 --> 00:00:46.334
and authority interpretation.

17
00:00:46.414 --> 00:00:50.598
Fragments are not sent in an HTTP request
and should not influence destination

18
00:00:50.678 --> 00:00:51.122
policy.

19
00:00:51.202 --> 00:00:53.421
Treat the port as part of the destination.

20
00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:57.369
An allowed hostname does not imply that
every service port on that host is

21
00:00:57.449 --> 00:00:58.083
permitted.

22
00:00:58.163 --> 00:01:02.093
Normalize the absent port to the protocol
default for policy comparison,

23
00:01:02.173 --> 00:01:05.090
and allow only the specific ports the
operation needs.

24
00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.671
Compare hostname values exactly or apply
one deliberately defined subdomain rule.

25
00:01:09.751 --> 00:01:14.316
Authorizing trusted.example is different
from authorizing every controlled child

26
00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:18.771
beneath it, and neither rule should match
an unrelated name that only ends with the

27
00:01:18.851 --> 00:01:19.802
same characters.

28
00:01:19.882 --> 00:01:22.735
Structured parsing provides useful
normalization,

29
00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:26.492
including case handling and
internationalized domain processing,

30
00:01:26.572 --> 00:01:28.854
but it is not a complete security
decision.

31
00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:32.485
The parser may accept an IP literal, an
IPv6 host, an explicit port,

32
00:01:32.565 --> 00:01:36.813
or a perfectly valid public-looking
hostname whose DNS answers lead somewhere

33
00:01:36.893 --> 00:01:37.527
forbidden.

34
00:01:37.607 --> 00:01:41.284
Record the parsed, normalized components
for the next policy steps,

35
00:01:41.364 --> 00:01:43.773
while avoiding sensitive query data in
logs.

36
00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:48.228
URL parsing answers whether the input can
be interpreted under supported syntax.

37
00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:52.492
Destination authorization still requires
hostname policy, DNS resolution,

38
00:01:52.572 --> 00:01:56.820
address classification, redirect controls,
and an approved connection path.
